#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b46ce4-cfe0-5da6-bc91-65ae8c544393" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Through a Surface as a Double Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "9a1112c0-db94-5572-ba53-b78116b7c273" :question/math-academy-id "e-12355" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) =\\left\\langle x+y,\\: x-y,\\: xyz \\right\\rangle, \\quad \\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u^2,\\: v^2 ,\\: 1 \\right\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset \\mathbb{R}^2.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d7/d7b8d89cae048a9cd5e04299bebdcb3a5d5d21ac6d5a31c48482aa00ca71517c.svg)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\,x-y,\\,xyz⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,4uv⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198380 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f322cca7-7530-50bf-b262-89faa81f5906" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Through a Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "0364a11f-e567-517e-acf2-471708dbdc0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12358" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\left\\langle z, \\: x, \\: y \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle 1+2u+v, \\: u+v, \\: u-v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩|}_{x=1+2u+v\\,y=u+v\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\,1+2u+v,\\,u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u-v,\\,1+2u+v,\\,u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (-2)+(1+2u+v)\\cdot 3+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+2v+3+6u+3v+u+v \\\\  & =5u+6v+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u+6v+3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+6v+3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[5uv+3{v}^{2}+3v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5u+6)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2}+6u]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b46ce4-cfe0-5da6-bc91-65ae8c544393" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Through a Surface as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94132937-eda0-5e90-a5ac-7f0f9def7aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194136} {:answer/id #uuid "f7544775-c055-5b3c-895a-3510daf49d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194137} {:answer/id #uuid "45e9bfa2-b5ab-5fb6-8215-c0e7746dd7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194138} {:answer/id #uuid "47bb57ce-704e-5663-89a6-934af702d9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194139} {:answer/id #uuid "608267cb-3d68-525c-bb1c-ef2ceab4fd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94132937-eda0-5e90-a5ac-7f0f9def7aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194136} :answer-field/id #uuid "977eb979-4b50-5a03-b91d-aa19a8c0611f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6a0f062-0f76-50a3-a357-dfc6f1a08d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-160839" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xz,\\,2y,\\,1⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨uv,\\,u+v,\\,1-v⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}au{v}^{2}+b{v}^{2}+cuv-u+v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ v & 1 & 0 \\\\ u & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}v & 0 \\\\ u & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}v & 1 \\\\ u & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,v,v-u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨xz,\\,2y,\\,1⟩{|}_{x=uv\\,y=u+v\\,z=1-v} \\\\  & =⟨uv\\cdot (1-v),\\,2(u+v),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨uv-u{v}^{2},\\,2u+2v,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨uv-u{v}^{2},\\,2u+2v,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,v,\\,v-u⟩ \\\\  & =(uv-u{v}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+(2u+2v)\\cdot v+1\\cdot (v-u) \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}-uv+2uv+2{v}^{2}+v-u \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}+2{v}^{2}+uv-u+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot u{v}^{2}+2\\cdot {v}^{2}+1\\cdot uv-u+v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+1=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198380 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f322cca7-7530-50bf-b262-89faa81f5906" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Through a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a219dca-1e12-574f-9fc3-d709a89775ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421769} {:answer/id #uuid "4b502ee2-042d-5a3e-86ae-ba6ad14e00b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421770} {:answer/id #uuid "5be10e6b-2fc0-5157-8d4c-a6365644fb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421771} {:answer/id #uuid "ac272ec2-a123-566b-9ae4-94a591001c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421776} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4a967e-d128-5262-a38b-86976eb912e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b502ee2-042d-5a3e-86ae-ba6ad14e00b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421770} :answer-field/id #uuid "de26f158-7713-5413-99c7-ec6ea96881bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c963e37-fb2b-5cec-bc6e-5cbde0fb10aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161258" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3uv,\\,u,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩|}_{x=3uv\\,y=u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨3(3uv),\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9uv,\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨9uv,\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩ \\\\  & =9uv\\cdot (-1)+(-{u}^{2})\\cdot 3v+2\\cdot (-3u) \\\\  & =-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}v-{u}^{3}v-3{u}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{11}{2}v-3)\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{11}{4}{v}^{2}-3v]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{23}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b46ce4-cfe0-5da6-bc91-65ae8c544393" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Through a Surface as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189bee50-4a99-5b40-af14-d79a6be4cf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421767} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab31c93-1b65-5e8b-a761-9f8a56bd7091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421768} {:answer/id #uuid "936a7baa-180a-5ac4-87b7-254a0a451cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421773} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1d5f42-e69e-5804-8ffc-1ba7c0a90d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421774} {:answer/id #uuid "a21a5496-3418-514f-ae65-4dd4f8f20907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab31c93-1b65-5e8b-a761-9f8a56bd7091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421768} :answer-field/id #uuid "44498254-f6d6-5947-a33a-02e052e8e1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27b29e44-b12e-5311-b5aa-28b3cb92f7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161304" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,xy,\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2uv⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2v \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2v \\\\ 1 & 2u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,xy,\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=2uv} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,2uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,2uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-2v)+uv\\cdot (-2u)+2uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2{u}^{2}v-2{u}^{2}v+2uv \\\\  & =2uv-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\cdot uv+(-4)\\cdot {u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2-4=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198380 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f322cca7-7530-50bf-b262-89faa81f5906" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Through a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127c5717-261e-5aef-838e-be5fab62b6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421765} {:answer/id #uuid "176992fe-e63a-5f75-8661-ee649edc6971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421766} {:answer/id #uuid "794b5eb2-d0f5-57bd-8363-4337b7e59452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421772} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9f4e2e-e860-55d7-8d5c-c33be0190db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421778} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fec48c-2c4b-5a3b-8559-e99202ca21e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "127c5717-261e-5aef-838e-be5fab62b6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421765} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8009739-528a-5b9d-8b7a-977eb7894c6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3288098-3c26-556d-b6c9-f206da47adfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162593" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,{u}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-1,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩|}_{x=-v\\,y=-u\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,-2(-v),\\,-(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 0+2v\\cdot (-2u)+u\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4uv+u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{u}^{2}v+\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2v+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{[{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}v]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
